{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-81912",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Our goal is to express $V$ in terms of $r.$ Therefore, we need to isolate $h$ in the above equation, which we do using cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot r & =1\\cdot h \\\\ 4r & =h \\\\ h & =4r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting $h=4r$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 4r \\\\  & =4\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
